  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,-x+z=0}.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Solving $-x+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=x.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
